# States allowed to exceed the committed window budget, with the reason and the ceiling.
#
# Format:  <view>  <maximum allowed window height in pt>
#
# This is a RATCHET, read by scripts/window-fit-check.sh. A state listed here is already known to
# miss the budget; the number is how badly it is allowed to miss it. Raising a number to make the
# gate pass defeats the only thing the file does. Removing a line when the state starts fitting is
# the intended end state.
#
# content-starting — 661 pt against a 620 pt budget, over by 41.
#
#   Briefly recorded as 631 on 2026-08-20 and PUT BACK the same day. Removing the idle metrics
#   panel's floor appeared to take 30 pt off every state's minimum; it did not. It stopped the
#   window RESERVING room the panel still needed, so at the reported minimum the placeholder's
#   second line was cut in half. A smaller reported minimum is not automatically a better one.
#
#   All 41 pt are the run controls' disabled-reason block, which renders up to FOUR sentences that
#   all mean "a run is in progress": `disabledReasons` deduplicates by exact string rather than by
#   cause, so three differently-worded refusals from Start, Pause and Stop all draw, and the
#   pre-run controls' own sentence is deliberately separate from them. Collapsing them by cause is
#   worth ~80 pt and would clear the budget with 39 pt to spare.
#
#   NOT a sizing problem, which is why increment 7 did not take it: it changes what the app SAYS
#   during a run, and that is a product decision rather than a layout one. Recorded here so it is
#   visible on every run of the gate instead of living in a comment nobody opens.
#
#   `starting` is also transient — unmount, verify, acquire, read geometry — so the window grows
#   for a few seconds and shrinks back. That makes it less bad, not fine: macOS enforces a content
#   minimum, so a user at 600 pt on a small screen has the window jump when a run starts.
#
content-starting 661
